1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,870
Think about a list you already use:
contacts, places to

2
00:00:03,870 --> 00:00:07,810
visit, or resources for a community
activity. We will turn

3
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:10,930
a fictional resource list into a small
browser app that

4
00:00:10,930 --> 00:00:15,390
helps someone find an entry. Your
experience with lists gives

5
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:18,230
you a useful starting point for deciding
whether the result

6
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:22,390
makes sense. You do not need to become a
professional

7
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:26,490
programmer today. You will practice
directing a build, checking

8
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:30,710
what it does, and making one controlled
improvement. The first

9
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:34,510
version has one page, a search field, a
category filter,

10
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:39,110
and readable results. Keep the records
fictional so the work

11
00:00:39,110 --> 00:00:42,490
can be shared and tested without exposing
anyone's private information.

12
00:00:44,633 --> 00:00:48,193
A website can present information for
people to read. A

13
00:00:48,193 --> 00:00:51,753
web app responds to input, such as
filtering our resource

14
00:00:51,753 --> 00:00:56,333
list. Software as a service usually
provides an ongoing hosted

15
00:00:56,333 --> 00:01:00,993
service and may include accounts, stored
data, subscriptions, and

16
00:01:00,993 --> 00:01:05,493
operational support. Our one-file practice
app is not all of

17
00:01:05,493 --> 00:01:09,573
that. It has no real account system,
private database, or

18
00:01:09,573 --> 00:01:13,353
payment service. This distinction helps
you ask for an achievable

19
00:01:13,353 --> 00:01:18,073
first version. A convincing sign-in screen
does not prove secure

20
00:01:18,073 --> 00:01:22,273
authentication exists behind it. Start by
describing the useful

21
00:01:22,273 --> 00:01:26,153
behavior you can actually test in the
browser, then treat

22
00:01:26,153 --> 00:01:30,313
hosting, security, and service operations
as separate decisions

23
00:01:30,313 --> 00:01:31,893
that require more work.

24
00:01:33,761 --> 00:01:37,921
HTML describes the content and structure:
a heading, a labeled

25
00:01:37,921 --> 00:01:42,461
search field, and a list of resources. CSS
controls how

26
00:01:42,461 --> 00:01:46,761
those parts are arranged and displayed.
JavaScript responds to input,

27
00:01:47,001 --> 00:01:51,741
such as deciding which records match a
search. Think of

28
00:01:51,741 --> 00:01:55,021
a familiar form: the questions and fields
are its structure,

29
00:01:55,861 --> 00:01:58,761
their presentation helps you read them,
and the rules determine

30
00:01:58,761 --> 00:02:02,041
what happens when you use it. The analogy
is a

31
00:02:02,041 --> 00:02:05,761
starting point, not a complete description
of software. Open the

32
00:02:05,761 --> 00:02:08,841
supplied starter in the browser and
identify a visible example

33
00:02:08,841 --> 00:02:12,321
of each part. Changing a heading is
different from changing

34
00:02:12,321 --> 00:02:13,881
the rule that filters the list.

35
00:02:15,783 --> 00:02:20,843
Define success in terms someone else can
observe. First, searching

36
00:02:20,843 --> 00:02:25,523
for library should show the fictional
Community library entry. Second,

37
00:02:25,823 --> 00:02:29,863
searching for an unmatched term should
show a clear no-results

38
00:02:29,863 --> 00:02:34,283
message. Third, the controls should work
with the keyboard. Add

39
00:02:34,283 --> 00:02:37,383
a check for different letter cases and a
narrow screen.

40
00:02:37,943 --> 00:02:41,543
These are acceptance checks: statements
about behavior that help

41
00:02:41,543 --> 00:02:45,023
you decide whether the result is usable.
Avoid a requirement

42
00:02:45,023 --> 00:02:48,363
such as make it amazing, because it does
not explain

43
00:02:48,363 --> 00:02:51,923
what to test. Pause and write three checks
for a

44
00:02:51,923 --> 00:02:55,283
small app idea of your own. Ask a partner
whether

45
00:02:55,283 --> 00:02:58,503
they could carry out each check without
guessing what you meant.

46
00:03:00,290 --> 00:03:04,150
A useful prompt describes the user, the
task, the controls,

47
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:09,010
the data boundaries, and the checks. For
example: build one

48
00:03:09,010 --> 00:03:13,090
local HTML page that searches fictional
community resources by name

49
00:03:13,090 --> 00:03:18,110
and category. Include labeled fields,
visible keyboard focus, a

50
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:22,910
no-results message, and no external
dependencies. Keep all example

51
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:27,730
records fictional and explain how to run
the file. Ask

52
00:03:27,730 --> 00:03:30,450
the AI to describe the important parts so
you can

53
00:03:30,450 --> 00:03:33,810
inspect its work. Do not put passwords or
private API

54
00:03:33,810 --> 00:03:37,270
keys into the prompt or the browser code.
People can

55
00:03:37,270 --> 00:03:40,750
inspect code delivered to their browser. A
longer prompt is

56
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:44,290
not automatically better; each instruction
should clarify behavior

57
00:03:44,290 --> 00:03:45,950
or a boundary you can verify.

58
00:03:47,978 --> 00:03:51,438
Use the instructor-approved tool if one is
available, or begin

59
00:03:51,438 --> 00:03:55,238
with the supplied starter. Save the file
with its HTML

60
00:03:55,238 --> 00:03:59,018
extension and open it in the browser.
Confirm that the

61
00:03:59,018 --> 00:04:04,938
heading, search field, filter, and results
appear. Keep this first

62
00:04:04,938 --> 00:04:09,138
working version before asking for changes.
If no AI account

63
00:04:09,138 --> 00:04:12,418
is available, you can still complete the
exercise with a

64
00:04:12,418 --> 00:04:16,418
plain-text editor: change the heading and
one fictional resource,

65
00:04:17,138 --> 00:04:20,838
save the file, and refresh the browser. Be
careful that

66
00:04:20,838 --> 00:04:24,818
the editor has not silently added a
text-file extension. The

67
00:04:24,818 --> 00:04:27,818
goal is a working result you can explain,
not evidence

68
00:04:27,818 --> 00:04:30,678
that you used a particular paid service or
model.

69
00:04:32,647 --> 00:04:35,487
Type library into the search field and
compare the result

70
00:04:35,487 --> 00:04:39,387
with your first acceptance check. Then
enter an unmatched term

71
00:04:39,387 --> 00:04:43,027
such as zzz. The page should explain that
there are

72
00:04:43,027 --> 00:04:46,567
no matching resources rather than
appearing broken or showing an

73
00:04:46,567 --> 00:04:50,547
old result. Clear the field and try
LIBRARY in uppercase.

74
00:04:51,767 --> 00:04:55,707
If the intended behavior is a
case-insensitive search, it should

75
00:04:55,707 --> 00:04:59,187
find the same record. Next combine the
search with a

76
00:04:59,187 --> 00:05:03,147
category filter and inspect whether both
conditions apply. Record

77
00:05:03,147 --> 00:05:07,387
expected and actual results separately. A
confident statement from

78
00:05:07,387 --> 00:05:10,447
the AI is not test evidence. The evidence
is what

79
00:05:10,447 --> 00:05:13,287
happened when you carried out a named
check on the saved file.

80
00:05:15,227 --> 00:05:17,887
Set the mouse aside and move through the
controls with

81
00:05:17,887 --> 00:05:22,167
the keyboard. Can you see which control
has focus? Can

82
00:05:22,167 --> 00:05:26,187
you enter a search and operate the filter?
Narrow the

83
00:05:26,187 --> 00:05:30,067
browser window and check that labels,
results, and buttons remain

84
00:05:30,067 --> 00:05:34,147
readable without important content being
cut off. Then ask where

85
00:05:34,147 --> 00:05:38,687
the data lives. In our starter, fictional
records are inside

86
00:05:38,687 --> 00:05:43,147
the file. Browser storage, if added later,
may remain only

87
00:05:43,147 --> 00:05:45,987
in that browser and may be cleared; it is
not

88
00:05:45,987 --> 00:05:49,927
automatically a shared database or a
backup. Test persistence rather

89
00:05:49,927 --> 00:05:54,507
than assuming it. Use the course's
existing text-size and accessibility

90
00:05:54,507 --> 00:05:57,547
habits as standards for the app you build.

91
00:05:59,316 --> 00:06:03,516
Suppose the uppercase search failed. A
useful revision request names

92
00:06:03,516 --> 00:06:08,016
the observed problem, the expected
behavior, and the check: LIBRARY

93
00:06:08,016 --> 00:06:12,816
returns no result, but library returns
Community library; make both

94
00:06:12,816 --> 00:06:16,196
searches match the same entry and preserve
the category filter.

95
00:06:17,216 --> 00:06:20,376
Save the revised version under a distinct
name. Repeat the

96
00:06:20,376 --> 00:06:25,516
failed check, then repeat a check that
previously passed. This

97
00:06:25,516 --> 00:06:29,196
second step can reveal a regression, where
fixing one behavior

98
00:06:29,196 --> 00:06:32,776
breaks another. If the new version is
worse, return to

99
00:06:32,776 --> 00:06:36,336
the saved working version. Pause and make
one small improvement

100
00:06:36,336 --> 00:06:39,556
or repair. Keep a brief test log so a
partner

101
00:06:39,556 --> 00:06:42,435
can follow what changed and why you trust
it.

102
00:06:43,985 --> 00:06:46,185
Show a partner the saved app and carry out
your

103
00:06:46,185 --> 00:06:50,445
three acceptance checks. Explain one part
of the page, one

104
00:06:50,445 --> 00:06:55,565
change you made, and one limitation that
remains. A local

105
00:06:55,565 --> 00:06:58,625
fictional prototype can be a successful
learning result without

106
00:06:58,625 --> 00:07:03,365
being ready to operate a public service.
Hosting is a

107
00:07:03,365 --> 00:07:07,305
separate choice. A real service handling
accounts, private data,

108
00:07:07,305 --> 00:07:11,845
or payments needs appropriate security,
access controls, support,

109
00:07:12,445 --> 00:07:16,305
and testing beyond this exercise. Do not
treat a decorative

110
00:07:16,305 --> 00:07:20,685
sign-in form as protection. Carry forward
the method you practiced:

111
00:07:20,685 --> 00:07:25,285
define a small task, give clear
instructions, inspect the result,

112
00:07:26,145 --> 00:07:28,925
test more than the easiest path, and keep
a way

113
00:07:28,925 --> 00:07:30,165
back when you revise it.
